  "assumptions": [
    "Idealized small-swing pendulum: period T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s², exact for the model and only approximately true of a real pendulum.",
    "The string is light and doesn't stretch, and the bob is a point mass at the centre of the sphere. There is no air resistance or friction, so the swing never dies down.",
    "The swing amplitude is fixed at 10°. The real period is about 0.2% longer at this angle, and that difference is not shown.",
    "The swing phase is worked out from the timeline time, so changing the length makes the bob jump to the position the new period gives at that moment. The 60 s loop also restarts the swing."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "const g=9.81,A=10*Math.PI/180;\nexport function evaluate({inputs,time}){const L=inputs.length;const T=2*Math.PI*Math.sqrt(L/g);const th=A*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*time/T);return{nodes:{swing:{quaternion:[0,0,Math.sin(th/2),Math.cos(th/2)]},strN:{position:[0,-L/2,0],scale:[1,L,1]},bobN:{position:[0,-L,0]}},readouts:{period:T,len:L}};}"
  }
